      sectionTitle: "Qur'anic Guidance on Loans and Debts; The Substance of Loans",
      title: "Qur'anic Guidance on Documentation, and What Can Validly Be Loaned",
      overview: "The Qur'an (2:282-283) instructs documenting and witnessing credit transactions; the substance of a valid Qard is anything Mubah (permissible), valuable, and available in kind for repayment, with schools differing on whether nonfungibles may be loaned.",
      simpleExplanation: "The Qur'an tells people to write down and get witnesses for deals involving future payment (like loans), while everyday spot transactions don't need this. As for what can actually be lent under Qard, it has to be something useful and permissible that has a like-for-like substitute available -- so you can pay back an equivalent. Scholars differ on whether you can lend things that aren't 'identical units,' like a specific animal, but the Prophet did lend/borrow animals, so most schools allow it.",
      academicExplanation: "Qur'an 2:282-283 instructs believers, when dealing in lending or future-obligation transactions, to reduce the terms to writing via a faithful scribe, have the liable party dictate the terms without diminishing what he owes (or his guardian dictate if he is deficient/weak), and secure two male witnesses (or one man and two women). The verse distinguishes documented credit transactions (future payment) from spot transactions (no writing required, though oral witnesses/trust between parties suffice); if traveling without a scribe, a pledge with possession (Rihn Maqbudah) may serve the purpose. This covers Salam (advance payment, future delivery) and Bai' Mu'ajjal (future payment) transactions, and reflects the Qur'an's guidance toward the highest standard of commercial morality, as though every transaction were conducted in the presence of God. On the substance of loans (7.6): besides Thaman-type goods, Qard can be given in anything Mubah (permissible), of value, with a similar available for repayment. The Hanafi school restricts loans to fungibles (similars by weight/measure), though the Sunnah shows the Prophet accepted Qard of animals (nonfungibles); the other three schools (Maliki, Shafi'e, Hanbali) allow Qard of any saleable commodity except human beings, provided the amount/value of the loan is known without doubt. The Maliki school specifically defines Qard as a loan of something 'valuable,' granted only as a favour (distinguishing it from 'Ariyah or Hibah), to be recovered in the same form granted -- the term 'valuable' excludes worthless items, and 'favour' clarifies the benefit accrues to the borrower alone, not the lender. Under this view, Qard may consist of merchandise, animals, or similars of weight -- essentially anything that could be the subject of Salam.",
      examExplanation: "Qur'an 2:282-283: document + witness (2 men, or 1 man + 2 women) future-obligation/credit transactions; spot transactions need no writing. Traveling without a scribe: a pledge with possession (Rihn Maqbudah) suffices. Applies to Salam and Bai' Mu'ajjal. Substance of loans: Hanafi -- Qard restricted to fungibles (similars by weight/measure) only; other 3 schools (Maliki/Shafi'e/Hanbali) -- Qard allowed for any saleable item except humans, provided amount/value is known without doubt. Maliki definition of Qard emphasizes 'valuable' (excludes worthless items) and 'favour' (benefit is for the borrower alone).",
