      id: "ch7-t4",
      sectionNumber: "7.7",
      sectionTitle: "Repayment of the Principal Only; Time Value of Money in Loans and Debts",
      title: "Repayment of Principal Only, and Why Time Value Doesn't Apply to Loans",
      overview: "A loan must be repaid at exactly its principal amount, with no exemptions; unlike in trade, no value can be assigned to the time given for payment of a Qard or Dayn once the amount is mutually fixed.",
      simpleExplanation: "Borrowing is a normal part of life and is allowed, but debt is serious -- it must always be repaid, with no shortcuts, and demanding anything above the exact amount borrowed is forbidden, no matter who's involved (Muslim or non-Muslim, employer or employee, government or citizen). Unlike in ordinary trading, where a credit price can legitimately be higher than a cash price, once a loan amount is fixed, you can't add anything extra just because time passed before repayment -- money is only exchanged equally for the same money.",
      academicExplanation: "Lending/borrowing is unavoidable in human life and therefore permissible -- the Prophet himself borrowed both for the Islamic State and privately -- though Islam discourages borrowing for lavish consumption. A loan must be paid; debt is not forgiven even for martyrs. A loan whereby anything in excess of the principal is exacted becomes unlawful (Riba), with no exemption based on the parties' identities (Muslim/non-Muslim, employer/employee, State/people). Prohibition of Riba means money can be lent with no expectation of return above the principal; every loan drawing forth or stipulating profit is unlawful. The Prophet said a creditor must refrain from even accepting a gift from a borrower after the loan, unless such gift exchange was already customary between them before the loan; however, indirect, costless customary benefits (e.g. repaying the debt in a different country if convenient for both parties, as Ibn Zubair did via drafts on his brother in Iraq, unopposed by Ibn Abbas and Ali) are permissible. On time value of money (7.8): while a credit price may legitimately differ from a cash price in trading, no value can be assigned to the time given for payment of a receivable once its amount is mutually stipulated in a Qard or Dayn context -- because money is not a lawful commodity for homogeneous exchange except in equal sums (e.g. $100 must be exchanged for exactly $100). Time valuation is approved by the Shari'ah in business/trade (as detailed in Chapter 4) but not in Qard or Dayn, which are virtuous acts from which no benefit may be taken. What is prohibited specifically is claiming time value of money as a predetermined quantity at a predetermined rate, unrelated to real-sector business. Currency notes are all factually homogeneous (representing purchasing power, being legal tender of the same genus/'Illah, just as dinars and dirhams were); therefore a person avoiding Riba cannot sell or lease currency notes as if they were ordinary goods/assets. Government securities, bonds, and savings certificates similarly command value like gold/silver and are thus 'money'; while gold/silver may serve as both Thaman and Mabi', bonds/currency notes reflect value only and cannot serve as Mabi' (they have no value absent government authority).",
      examExplanation: "A loan (Qard/Dayn) must be repaid at exactly the principal amount -- no exemptions based on the parties involved. A creditor may not even accept a gift from a borrower post-loan unless such gifting predates the loan and was already customary (indirect, costless customary benefits like cross-border repayment convenience ARE allowed). Time value of money: recognized in TRADE (credit price ≠ cash price is fine), but NOT in Qard/Dayn -- once a loan/debt amount is fixed, no additional value can attach to the delay in payment; $100 must be repaid as exactly $100. Currency notes cannot be sold/leased like ordinary goods since they're homogeneous, purchasing-power-representing legal tender.",
