        "Duyun Halah (already-due, demandable debt): remission of part for early payment of the rest IS allowed by nearly all jurists (as long as not made a precondition) -- delay is not the debtor's right here.",
        "Duyun Mu'ajjalah (debt with a settled future due date, not yet due): remission for early payment is NOT allowed -- treated as Riba (Imam Malik's clear position).",
        "The Miqdad bin Aswad case (accepting 90 dirhams for a 100-dirham not-yet-due debt) was explicitly condemned by the Prophet as involving Riba for both parties.",
        "Some later Hanafi jurists permit remitting the remaining-period profit margin in Murabaha-Mu'ajjal specifically (where the margin was tied to the credit period) on early payment or death -- but most contemporary scholars reject this for bank Murabaha as too interest-like.",
        "AAOIFI: a CONTRACTUAL prepayment rebate commitment is prohibited (Murabaha price must be fixed once and for all), but a discretionary rebate, given solely at the bank's own choice with no prior commitment, is permitted."
