      overview: "Traditional and the overwhelming majority of contemporary jurists, including the OIC Islamic Fiqh Council (with Malaysian representation), unanimously prohibit selling debt instruments at other than par value, contrary to some Malaysian Shari'ah practice for Sukuk secondary trading.",
      simpleExplanation: "Some markets (particularly in Malaysia) allow trading debt-based Islamic bonds at a discount or premium in secondary markets. But most scholars worldwide, and even the international body representing all Islamic countries (including Malaysia), say this isn't allowed -- you can't sell a debt for more or less than its exact face value, even if it's technically backed by real assets.",
      academicExplanation: "Secondary market trading of debt and debt-based securities is technically possible through Bai' al-Dayn, as seen with a variety of Malaysia-based Sukuk. However, Jamhoor Ulama (the mainstream body of jurists) reject this, even where the underlying Sukuk debt is asset-supported. Traditional Muslim jurists are unanimous that Bai' al-Dayn at a discount or premium (rather than par/face value) is not allowed in Shari'ah, and the overwhelming majority of contemporary Shari'ah scholars share this view. Some Malaysian experts have permitted this kind of sale, typically citing the Shafi'e school's ruling -- but this citation is misapplied, since Shafi'e jurists in fact allowed debt sale only where the debt was sold AT its par value, not at a discount or premium. Rosly and Sanusi (1999) note that trading Islamic bonds at a discount via Bai' al-Dayn has been found unacceptable by the Jumhur Ulama, including al-Shafi'e himself, and that Malaysian Islamic bonds' position remains unacceptable to Middle Eastern jurists even though some Malaysian jurists hold otherwise. The OIC Islamic Fiqh Council -- with representation from all Islamic countries, including Malaysia -- has approved the prohibition of Bai' al-Dayn unanimously, without a single dissent.",
