  chapterSummary: "Chapter 7 sets out the Shari'ah law of loan and debt, distinguishing Qard, Salaf, Dayn and 'Ariyah, and establishing the central rule that a loan must be repaid at exactly its principal, since any stipulated benefit above it -- in quantity or quality -- is Riba. It shows how this rule shapes both conventional and Islamic banking structures (deposits and current accounts as Qard), explains why time value of money applies in trade but not in loans, and details the Shari'ah's instructions to debtors and creditors, including the narrow, discretion-only scope of Husnal Qadha and the technical rules on remitting part of an already-due debt. It then covers how default is handled today (charity-bound penalty clauses plus actual-loss compensation for Dayn, never for Qard) and how insolvency is resolved. Finally, it works through the mechanisms of Hawalah (assignment of debt, preserving recourse) and Kafalah/Rihn (guarantee and pledge), and closes with two firm prohibitions: selling debt at other than par value (Bai' al-Dayn) and indexing loans/debts to inflation, currencies, or commodities.",
  keyTakeaways: [
    "Qard (demand loan, similar item repaid) and Dayn (any credit-transaction debt) must be repaid at exactly the principal; 'Ariyah requires returning the exact borrowed item.",
    "Any loan condition creating a benefit above principal -- in quantity or quality -- is void and constitutes Riba; conventional bank deposits and current accounts are classified as Qard for this reason.",
    "Time value of money is recognized in genuine trade (credit price may exceed cash price) but never in Qard or Dayn repayment.",
    "Debtors must repay promptly (wilful default is injustice); creditors are urged toward leniency but cannot be compelled to extend time or waive debt.",
    "Husnal Qadha (voluntary excess repayment) is commendable but must never be systematized; remission for early payment is allowed only for already-due debts (Duyun Halah), not not-yet-due debts (Duyun Mu'ajjalah).",
    "Penalty clauses for wilful default are now permitted (proceeds to charity) for Dayn but never for Qard; actual-loss compensation (not opportunity cost) may also be claimed via court/arbitration.",
    "Hawalah (assignment of debt) is permissible because it preserves recourse to the original debtor; a pure sale of debt lacks recourse and is prohibited for Gharar/Riba.",
    "Kafalah (suretyship) and Rihn (pledge) are the two guarantee mechanisms; the pledger retains the pledged property's risk and reward, and 'pledge cannot be foreclosed' without court process.",
    "Bai' al-Dayn (selling debt at other than par value) is unanimously prohibited, and indexing loans/debts to inflation, gold, or currency baskets is likewise rejected as a form of Riba."
  ]
